{"body": "Conjecture: The function is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading is: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The two reply texts are:\n\nReply A:\n```python\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}\n\nReply B:\n{\"b\": 2}\n\nThe rule requires Reply A to be scored as {\"a\": 1}, because it is the last object inside a code fence (the fence is marked python, but the rule says any code fence). The rule requires Reply B to be scored as {\"b\": 2}, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The check recover_json_object, however, gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\": 2} for each, because its fence regex only matches fences with no language tag or with json/JSON, so the python fence is not recognized as a fence, and the object inside it is treated as a top-level object; the last top-level object in Reply A is {\"b\": 2}. What would make me wrong: if recover_json_object actually returns {\"a\": 1} for Reply A (for example, if the fence regex were changed to match any language tag, or if the top-level scan excluded fenced content), then the two replies would be told apart as the rule requires."}